# Jenkins CI often executes from `main`

Many Jenkins multibranch / Pipeline-from-SCM setups load the `Jenkinsfile`
(or pipeline entrypoint) from `main` (or another tracked default branch), or
from the branch under build only after it is pushed. Working-tree-only edits
have **no effect** until SCM has the commit the job checks out.

## Why

SCM-driven Jenkins checks out a specific ref. A pipeline change is “live”
only for builds that check out a commit containing it. Folder jobs that
point at a fixed branch (often `main`) ignore feature-branch Jenkinsfiles
entirely. Multibranch / PR jobs run the Jenkinsfile from the head under
test — but only after push (or the SCM polling / webhook that creates the
run).

## Rule

When you change a `Jenkinsfile` or pipeline entrypoint:

1. Follow `jenkins-pipeline` (and skill `jenkins-pipeline-author` when
   authoring or reviewing). YAML entrypoints: 2-space indent, quote
   ambiguous scalars, no secret values in the file.
2. Commit related files together (pipeline plus scripts it calls) when the
   user asks to commit.
3. Remind the user that the job must see the commit on the branch it builds
   (often default for some folder jobs; head branch for multibranch PR jobs).
4. Do **not** commit or push automatically unless the consuming project has
   an explicit preference for that this turn.
5. After a run that could see the new file, verify with `jenkins-builds`
   (fresh `get_build` / console). Do not claim “pipeline fixed” from the
   editor alone.

## Branch matrix (say which applies)

| Setup | What must hold the edit |
| --- | --- |
| Pipeline job → fixed branch | That branch (often `main` / `master`) |
| Multibranch / org folder | The branch or PR head under build |
| Lightweight checkout / Script Path | The SCM path + ref the job config names |
| Replay / inline script in UI | Out of band — prefer SCM; say so if UI-only |

If you cannot tell which setup the job uses, say so and suggest reading the
job config (`get_item_config`) or the job’s SCM section — do not invent the
tracked branch.

## Pairing with builds

1. Edit pipeline files → remind about SCM pickup.
2. User merges / pushes → trigger or wait for the job they named.
3. Read build result + short console evidence.
4. Only then call the pipeline change verified.

## Refuse

- Do not silently amend history or force-push to make CI pick up an edit.
- Do not rewrite job config to point at a different branch unless the user
  explicitly asked for that config change.
- Do not claim a local unsaved buffer is what Jenkins executed.
